      "lineNbr": 185,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-01-10T21:01:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. Need to check that the file is a regular file before using the wrapped key.\n2. Need to check that the file is using inline encryption before using the wrapped key.\n3. Need to check that the key is actually a wrapped key.\n4. There\u0027s no need to use the on-stack buffer for the wrapped key.\n5. This code allows the same wrapped key to be used for multiple encryption modes.  I think we should explicitly prevent that, unless the hardware is expected to always handle that appropriately by deriving a different key for each mode.\n\nCan you consider something like:\n\n        if (S_ISREG(inode-\u003ei_mode) \u0026\u0026 mk-\u003emk_secret.is_wrapped_key) {\n                int i;\n\n                if (!fscrypt_using_inline_encryption(ci)) {\n                        fscrypt_warn(ci-\u003eci_inode,\n                                     \"wrapped keys require inline encryption (-o inlinecrypt)\");\n                        err \u003d -EINVAL;\n                        goto out_unlock;\n                }\n\n                for (i \u003d 0; i \u003c\u003d __FSCRYPT_MODE_MAX; i++) {\n                        if (fscrypt_is_key_prepared(\u0026keys[mode_num], ci)) {\n                                fscrypt_warn(ci-\u003eci_inode,\n                                             \"wrapped keys only support one encryption mode at a time\");\n                                err \u003d -EINVAL;\n                                goto out_unlock;\n                        }\n                }\n                err \u003d fscrypt_prepare_key(prep_key, mk-\u003emk_secret.raw,\n                                          mk-\u003emk_secret.size, ci);\n                if (err)\n                        goto out_unlock;\n                goto done_unlock;\n        }",
      "range": {
